{"body":"## Finding\n\nTen records whose **label explicitly names a counterion or says \"salt\"** carry a\nmapping whose **recorded structure is a net-charged ion**. Seven are graded\n`EXACT_MATCH` — the strongest identity claim available, asserting that a salt\n*is* its anion.\n\n| net charge | identifier | quality | label | term (verified OLS4) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| −2 | CHEBI:16810 | **EXACT_MATCH** | `Na2 alpha-ketoglutarate` | `2-oxoglutarate(2-)` |\n| −2 | CHEBI:29988 | **EXACT_MATCH** | `Na-glutamate` | `L-glutamate(2-)` |\n| −2 | CHEBI:30924 | **EXACT_MATCH** | `Na-tartrate` | `L-tartrate(2-)` |\n| −1 | CHEBI:29032 | **EXACT_MATCH** | `Ca-pantothenate` | `(R)-pantothenate` |\n| −1 | CHEBI:35899 | **EXACT_MATCH** | `Na-crotonate` | `crotonate` |\n| +1 | CHEBI:46020 | **EXACT_MATCH** | `Tetramethyl ammonium` | (cation) |\n| −2 | cas:12208-13-8 | FALLBACK_REGISTRY | `potassium hexahydroxoantimonate` | — |\n| −1 | cas:207511-06-6 | NARROW_MATCH | `L-Malic acid disodium salt monohydrate` | — |\n| +1 | cas:9067-32-7 | NARROW_MATCH | `Hyaluronic acid sodium salt from S. equi` | — |\n| +1 | kgmicrobe.compound:… | CLOSE_MATCH | `Tetramethyl ammonium chloride` | — |\n\nAll five CHEBI labels above verified live against OLS4; none obsolete.\n\n## Why these are not covered by the #213 convention\n\nMIM deliberately grounds **bare anion labels** (`Fumarate`, `Citrate`, `Lactate`)\nto the fully-deprotonated CHEBI class, and that is correct — a bare label carries\nno counterion, and the anion is the species present at growth-medium pH.\n\nThese ten are a different case: the label **names the counterion**\n(`Na2 …`, `Ca-…`, `… sodium salt`). Sodium glutamate and glutamate(2−) are\ndifferent substances with different formulas, masses and CAS numbers. So the\nconvention does not license `EXACT_MATCH` here, and the anion mapping — while a\nreasonable *close/narrow* relation — overstates as identity.\n\nNote also `Na-glutamate`: monosodium glutamate is the **mono**-anion salt, so\neven the charge state of the current target is off by one.\n\n## Detection\n\nMechanical and high-precision. Net charge must be computed across **both** InChI\nlayers — `/q` alone gives 119 false positives, because a neutral salt like AlCl3\nis written `/q+3;;;/p-3`:\n\n```python\nnet = sum(component /q charges) + /p          # non-zero  => recorded structure is an ion\ndefect = net != 0 and label names a counterion or \"salt\"\n```\n\nThat narrows 119 → 40 → **10**, with no false positives in the 10 on inspection.\n\n## Suggested action\n\nPer record: either re-ground to a CHEBI salt term where one exists, or keep the\nanion term and downgrade `mapping_quality` to `CLOSE_MATCH`/`NARROW_MATCH` with\nthe reason recorded. The `EXACT_MATCH` grade is the part that is indefensible.\n\nWorth adding the check to the validation suite so a future promotion sweep\ncannot reintroduce it.\n\n## Provenance\n\nSurfaced by the Claude research lane on `1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium_Lysine`\n(CHEBI:61326 is the [C2mim]⁺ cation alone; the ingredient is a neutral salt),\nthen generalised repo-wide. Related: #312 (ledger triage), #309 (the two-lane\nharness).","comments":[{"id":"IC_kwDORhCjFc8AAAABN5YVuQ","author":{"login":"realmarcin"},"authorAssociation":"MEMBER","body":"## Escalation: for at least 4 of the 10, the remedy is a **merge**, not a re-grade\n\nI proposed \"re-ground to a salt term where one exists, or downgrade the grade\".\nFor several of these the correct salt term is not merely available in ChEBI — a\n**correctly-mapped live MIM record already holds it**. These are duplicates.\n\n| defect record | currently mapped to | correct sibling **already in the repo** |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Ca-pantothenate` (CHEBI:29032, EXACT_MATCH) | `(R)-pantothenate` (anion) | **`CHEBI:31345 Calcium pantothenate`**, live MAPPED |\n| `Na-glutamate` (CHEBI:29988, EXACT_MATCH) | `L-glutamate(2-)` (anion) | **`CHEBI:64243 Sodium L-glutamate`**, live MAPPED |\n| `Na-tartrate` (CHEBI:30924, EXACT_MATCH) | `L-tartrate(2-)` (anion) | **`CHEBI:63017 Sodium tartrate`**, live MAPPED |\n| `Tetramethyl ammonium` (CHEBI:46020, EXACT_MATCH) | the cation | `kgmicrobe.compound:tetramethyl_ammonium_chloride` — but the chloride is a *different* substance, so this one is a genuine re-grade, not a merge |\n\n## The pantothenate case shows how these survived\n\nThree surface forms of one substance exist, and the earlier merge pass caught\nonly two of them:\n\n```\nCHEBI:31345  Calcium pantothenate      MAPPED     <- correct\nCHEBI:31345  Calcium D-Pantothenate    REJECTED   <- correctly tombstoned, MERGED_INTO (#226)\nCHEBI:29032  Ca-pantothenate           MAPPED     <- missed; grounded to the anion instead\n```\n\n`Ca-pantothenate` escaped the #226 merge because its abbreviated `Ca-` prefix\ndoes not lexically resemble `Calcium …`, and having escaped it, lexical matching\nthen grounded it to the nearest thing it could find — the anion.\n\nSo this defect class and the duplicate-detection gap are the **same root cause**:\nan abbreviated-counterion surface form (`Ca-`, `Na-`, `Na2 `) that neither the\nmerge pass nor the matcher recognises as naming a salt.\n\n## Revised suggested action\n\nPer record, in this order:\n1. **Merge** into the existing correct record where one exists (pantothenate,\n   glutamate, tartrate) — preserving the abbreviated form as a RAW_TEXT synonym,\n   which also inoculates future merge passes against the same miss.\n2. Re-ground to a ChEBI salt term where one exists but no MIM record does.\n3. Only then fall back to downgrading `mapping_quality`.\n\nWorth adding `Ca-`/`Na-`/`Na2 `/`K-` prefix expansion to the duplicate-detection\nnormaliser, since that is what let these through.\n\nFound by the Claude research lane on `Ca-pantothenate`, which was handed to it\nwithout any indication that #315 existed; it reached the defect independently\nfrom the record's own `cas_rn` contradicting its identifier, and then found the\nduplicate — which was not in my original report. Verified against the collection.","createdAt":"2026-08-08T18:39:25Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/315#issuecomment-5227550137","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDORhCjFc8AAAABN5e4lQ","author":{"login":"realmarcin"},"authorAssociation":"MEMBER","body":"## One record missed by the original filter — an 11th\n\n`1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium lysine` (primary identifier **`CHEBI:61326`**, which\nis the **cation alone**, `LEXICAL_MATCH`) belongs in the list above and was not\nin it.\n\nThe keyword filter looked for `salt|sodium|potassium|calcium|…|hydrochloride` or\na `Na-`/`Ca-` prefix. An ionic liquid named `[cation] [anion]` contains none of\nthose words, so it escaped — the label is a pure cation+anion pair with no\ncounterion keyword at all.\n\nWidening the filter to cation-name endings\n(`imidazolium|pyridinium|ammonium|phosphonium|cholinium|sulfonium|tetrazolium`)\nfinds **exactly one** additional record, this one. So the gap is real but small.\n\n## Two near-misses that are correctly *not* defects\n\nBoth were checked and both are fine, which is worth recording so they are not\n\"fixed\" later:\n\n- **`1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium acetate`** (`cas:143314-17-4`) — its InChI is\n  `/q+1;/p-1`, which nets to **zero**: a properly encoded neutral salt. It is\n  `cas:`-primary with a `NARROW_MATCH` to the cation, which is the documented\n  legitimate pattern, not an identity over-claim.\n- **`2-Mercaptopyridine N-oxide sodium salt`** — `/q-1;+1`, also net zero.\n\nBoth confirm the two design decisions behind the detector: net charge must be\nsummed across the `/q` **and** `/p` layers (a first-component-only read flagged\n119 records, most of them neutral salts), and severity must key on the **primary\nidentifier's** prefix rather than on `ontology_mapping.ontology_id`, or every\n`cas:`-primary record carrying a narrow ontology match gets flagged for a claim\nit never made.\n\nFound when a research agent, asked to check whether the acetate repeated the\nlysine defect, verified it did **not** rather than assuming the pattern held.","createdAt":"2026-08-08T19:06:52Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/315#issuecomment-5227657365","viewerDidAuthor":true}],"number":315,"state":"CLOSED","title":"Salt-named records mapped EXACT_MATCH to the bare anion (10 records)","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/315"}
